<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ziggurat of Ur</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziggurat">Ziggurat</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a> is a great stepped temple tower at the ancient city of Ur in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, dedicated to the moon god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanna">Nanna</a>. It was built by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur-nammu">Ur-Nammu</a> and completed by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shulgi">Shulgi</a> in the twenty-first century BCE. Partially restored in modern times, it is one of the best-preserved ziggurats and a lasting monument of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neo-sumerian-empire">Neo-Sumerian Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ziggurat-of-ur.jpg" alt="Ziggurat of Ur" width="320" height="140" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tla2006 at English Wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ziggarat_of_Ur_001.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
